window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-a292ca32ae057ed8"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"roles-and-workspace-permissions\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"_1\">角色与工作区权限</h1> <blockquote> <p>完整文档索引请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在页面 URL 后追加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取该文档的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p>不同设置分别控制组织 ChatGPT 体验的不同部分。在一个区域授予某人访问权，不会自动让其获得其他区域的访问权。本文说明六个控制边界如何协同工作；具体设置步骤请参阅文中的链接。</p> <p>在工作区设置中，<strong>Codex and Work Local</strong> 会把本地 Codex 和 Work 访问权限合并在 <strong>Allow members to use Codex and Work Locally</strong> 之下。另一些工作区会把 <strong>Codex Local</strong> 和 <strong>Work Local</strong> 分成独立部分。在后一种布局中，<strong>Allow members to use Codex locally</strong> 授予本地 Codex 访问权，<strong>Use Work locally</strong> 授予本地 Work 访问权。启用其中一个，不会同时授予另一个。这些标签代表工作区权限，而不是不同产品或客户端。令牌权限和凭据有效期限制可能显示在 <strong>Access tokens</strong> 部分，也可能显示在本地访问部分，具体取决于工作区。托管配置是另一层，用于约束这些客户端中受支持能力的运行时行为。功能和实际生效的要求可能因客户端与版本而异。</p> <p><a id=\"understand-the-control-boundaries\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">了解控制边界</h2> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>边界</th> <th>控制内容</th> <th>不控制的内容</th> <th>当前参考资料</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>ChatGPT 工作区</td> <td>成员资格、席位、内置管理角色，以及对受支持工作区功能的基于角色的访问</td> <td>本地智能体权限、Platform API 组织访问权，或已连接服务中的权限</td> <td><a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8266401-managing-members-seat-types-roles-and-access-in-chatgpt-enterprise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ChatGPT 工作区访问（需联网）</a>和 <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/11750701-rbac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RBAC（需联网）</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>本地客户端</td> <td>ChatGPT 桌面应用、Codex CLI 和 IDE 扩展中受管理能力的运行时行为，包括审批、文件系统和网络访问、权限配置文件及允许的集成</td> <td>ChatGPT 席位、功能或模型资格，或外部数据访问权</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">托管配置</a>和<a href=\"#/article/a-4d7a5dc78b3c591e\">权限</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Codex 云端</td> <td>使用托管 Codex 工作流以及用户可用云环境的资格</td> <td>本地运行时策略，或源系统授予的代码库权限</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1\">云环境</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Platform API</td> <td>API 身份验证任务的组织与项目成员资格、API 密钥、模型访问权、用量和账单</td> <td>ChatGPT 工作区成员资格、本地客户端访问权或 Codex 云端访问权</td> <td><a href=\"https://platform.openai.com/docs/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenAI API Platform（需联网）</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>插件</td> <td>插件可用性和安装、内含技能、连接器访问权及受支持的连接器操作</td> <td>已连接服务中的授权，或更广泛的本地和云运行时权限</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-20bc98306a44b420\">插件控制</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>已连接系统</td> <td>已验证账户在源系统中可访问哪些代码库、文件、消息和操作</td> <td>ChatGPT 工作区、插件、Codex 云端或 Platform API 资格</td> <td>已连接服务自身的管理与访问控制</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>请求必须通过所有适用的控制边界。例如，工作区访问权可以让插件对用户可用，但用户登录的账户能读取哪些数据，仍由已连接服务决定。受支持本地客户端的权限配置文件可以限制一次运行，但不能授予工作区功能或模型使用权。</p> <p><a id=\"assign-workspace-access\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">分配工作区访问权</h2> <p>ChatGPT 工作区管理会将产品访问权与管理权限分开。</p> <p><a id=\"understand-the-difference-between-a-seat-an-admin-role-and-a-custom-role\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_4\">区分席位、管理员角色和自定义角色</h3> <p>席位决定成员可以访问哪些产品界面。根据工作区套餐，可用席位类型可能包括 ChatGPT 席位和 Codex 席位。</p> <p>内置工作区角色决定管理权限。<strong>Owner</strong>（所有者）管理整个工作区的设置；<strong>Admin</strong>（管理员）管理受支持的操作和群组；<strong>Member</strong>（成员）没有管理权限；<strong>Analytics Viewer</strong>（分析查看者）可以访问工作区分析数据。</p> <p>自定义角色定义成员可以使用哪些受支持功能。它们不会取代席位或套餐资格，不会授予已连接系统中的权限，也不会更改本地运行时要求。</p> <p><a href=\"https://player.vimeo.com/video/1215495812\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">嵌入内容需联网查看：基于角色的访问控制演示</a></p> <p><a id=\"set-the-workspace-default-then-create-targeted-custom-roles\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_5\">先设置工作区默认值，再建立有针对性的自定义角色</h3> <p>只有工作区所有者可以配置基于角色的访问控制（RBAC）并创建自定义角色。工作区设置为符合条件的权限建立基准。工作区所有者可以在支持的情况下，通过群组或直接向单个成员分配自定义角色。群组可以手动管理，也可以通过 SCIM 同步；一个成员可以拥有多个自定义角色。</p> <p>对于符合条件的权限，<strong>Default</strong>（默认）会继承工作区设置，<strong>On</strong>（开启）会通过该角色授予访问权，<strong>Off</strong>（关闭）则不会通过该角色授予访问权。普通角色权限以累加方式生效：其他已分配角色仍可能授予访问权。请检查直接分配的角色，以及通过群组获得的角色。Lockdown Mode（锁定模式）和产品资格另行生效。可用权限状态可能因功能而异。</p> <p><a id=\"review-work-local-and-work-cloud-permissions\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"work-local-work-cloud\">检查 Work Local 和 Work Cloud 权限</h3> <p>请在 <strong>Admin Console &gt; Permissions &amp; roles &gt; Workspace capabilities &gt; Cloud computer capabilities</strong> 中配置 <strong>Cloud browser use</strong> 和 <strong>Cloud network access</strong>。Work Cloud 和 dots 都可以使用这些共享能力；它们可以独立于 Work Cloud 访问权进行配置。Work 任务仍需具备 Work 访问权，并获得使用所需每项能力的权限。浏览器访问与代码/Shell 网络访问应分别检查。禁用其中一项不会自动禁用另一项。</p> <p>如果工作区提供 <strong>Work Local</strong> 和 <strong>Work Cloud</strong>，请检查工作区默认设置和每个适用自定义角色。只有符合条件的工作区才能使用 Work；可用控制项可能因套餐、工作区配置和功能推出进度而异。角色不能扩大成员席位所允许的访问范围。</p> <p><strong>Work Cloud</strong> 控制受支持的 ChatGPT Work 云端任务。如果两个控制项彼此独立，启用 <strong>Work Local</strong> 但不启用 <strong>Work Cloud</strong>，可以让成员在 ChatGPT 桌面应用中本地工作，但不能开始云端任务。本地 Codex 访问权使用 <strong>Codex Local</strong> 中的 <strong>Allow members to use Codex locally</strong> 控制。更改 <strong>Use Work locally</strong> 不会影响本地 Codex 访问权，也不会替代本地运行时要求。</p> <p>另一些工作区会显示合并后的 <strong>Codex and Work Local</strong> 部分。在这种布局中，<strong>Allow members to use Codex and Work Locally</strong> 同时控制两种产品。</p> <p>关于当前资格和设置，请参阅 <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001275-chatgpt-work-and-codex\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ChatGPT Work 和 Codex（需联网）</a>。</p> <p>席位、角色和权限会随产品与套餐更新而变化，请通过帮助中心查看最新权限列表和设置步骤：</p> <ul> <li><a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8266401-managing-members-seat-types-roles-and-access-in-chatgpt-enterprise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">管理成员、席位类型、角色和访问权（需联网）</a></li> <li><a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/11750701-rbac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">配置基于角色的访问控制（需联网）</a></li> <li><a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/9083985-group-permissions-in-gpts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">管理群组（需联网）</a></li> </ul> <p><a id=\"review-codex-cloud-access-and-environment-administration\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"codex-cloud\">检查 Codex Cloud 访问权与环境管理</h3> <p>请分别检查以下权限：</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>权限</th> <th>控制内容</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><strong>在云端使用 Codex（Use Codex in the cloud）</strong></td> <td>在 Codex Cloud 中运行任务的访问权</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Manage workspace environments</strong></td> <td>创建和编辑与工作区共享的环境</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p><strong>Manage workspace environments</strong> 位于 <strong>Use Codex in the cloud</strong> 之下，需要先有 Cloud 访问权，且默认关闭。上线前请审查获得此权限的成员。拥有 Cloud 访问权的成员，即使没有环境管理权限，也可以创建和编辑自己的个人环境。访问共享环境本身并不代表有权更改其配置。</p> <p>请用有代表性的身份验证两种工作流：使用环境的成员，以及负责管理环境的人员。两者仍都需要连接代码库和服务的相应访问权。设置与共享方式请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1\">云环境</a>；云端策略规划请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-811aa7aeaf6b8cd4?anchor=step-5-configure-codex-cloud\">管理员推广指南</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"local-computer-access-with-work-cloud\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"work-cloud\">Work Cloud 本地计算机访问</h3> <p>工作区所有者可以为符合条件的用户启用 <strong>Allow local computer access</strong>。请为目标用户启用 Work Cloud。<strong>Allow local computer access</strong> 位于 Work Cloud 设置之下。你不需要启用 <strong>Use Codex locally on the ChatGPT desktop app</strong>。成员必须使用 ChatGPT 登录到目标工作区。如果任一云端策略启用了 <code>enforce_residency</code>，Work 和 dots 的 <strong>Allow local computer access</strong> 都会被禁用。这项保护措施不会配置工作区驻留，也不会单独关闭 Work Cloud 或 dots。</p> <p>启用同步前，请审查 Agent Security 中的本地执行要求。如果目前仅通过 MDM 下发策略，请在配置 Agent Security 前审查适用的本地策略顺序。本地执行时，MDM 和旧版托管设备要求优先级高于 Agent Security，系统要求文件优先级低于 Agent Security。启用托管策略后，受支持的 Global 策略约束云端编排；本地执行要求则约束连接电脑。Work 云容器继续遵循现有 Work Cloud 策略。</p> <p>使用策略 API 管理 Global 设置；使用 Agent Security 界面管理 Local 或 Codex Cloud 设置。迁移后，现有 Global API 工作流仍可使用。请测试脚本和 Terraform 集成，并确认策略分配和排序没有改变。</p> <p>设置并验证访问权限：</p> <ul> <li>为更广泛的组织保留合适的工作区默认值。</li> <li>将受支持的自定义角色分配给需要访问权的用户或群组。</li> <li>检查每个适用角色。在一个普通角色中关闭权限，不会移除其他角色授予的访问权。</li> <li>使用具有预期权限的成员测试访问。</li> </ul> <p>使用 Work Cloud 本地计算机访问的任务由云端协调，也可以通过已连接电脑执行本地步骤。对于企业版用户，应用内的 Local/Cloud 切换开关及其默认设置在功能上线时保持不变。同步权限不会授予席位、已连接应用访问权、操作系统权限或不受限制的设备访问权。本地 Codex 权限与行为仍然独立。有关策略范围，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">托管配置</a>；兼容要求请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-b498643ba7f3888a\">Work 管理员常见问题</a>。</p> <p>关闭 Work Cloud 本地计算机访问会中断当前正在运行的轮次。用户可以在已有云端对话中开始新一轮；新一轮会自动使用无法访问本地文件的 Work Cloud。关闭同步本身不会移除成员的工作区访问权。</p> <p><a id=\"control-computer-history-access\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"computer-history\">控制 Computer History 访问权</h3> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-f3a0216fc3d2956b\">Computer History</a> 在 Business 和 Enterprise 工作区中默认关闭。在工作区所有者明确授予访问权之前，成员无法启用它。Enterprise 工作区所有者可以按角色授予访问权：</p> <ol> <li>打开<a href=\"https://chatgpt.com/admin/settings\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Workspace Settings &gt; Permissions &amp; roles</strong>（需联网）</a>。</li> <li>找到 <strong>Computer History</strong>，选择应当获得访问权的工作区角色。</li> <li>为该角色打开 <strong>Enable Computer History</strong>。</li> </ol> <p>此权限只允许被分配的成员开启 Computer History，并不会替他们直接开启功能。每位成员都必须在 macOS 版 ChatGPT 桌面应用中自行选择加入，并可选择哪些应用和网站提供记录。没有所需工作区权限的成员无法通过本地设置启用此功能。</p> <p><a id=\"apply-local-runtime-policy\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_6\">应用本地运行时策略</h2> <p>本地运行时策略约束 ChatGPT 桌面应用、Codex CLI 和 IDE 扩展中的受管能力。云端托管要求还取决于是否使用受支持的 ChatGPT 登录方式，以及套餐资格。权限配置文件和托管要求可以限制命令、文件系统访问、网络访问、审批以及其他本地运行时行为，但不会改变用户的席位、工作区角色、模型资格或外部系统权限。</p> <p>在本地策略允许的范围内，用户可以选择内置或自定义权限配置文件。管理员可以通过受支持的托管配置渠道下发默认值和要求。有关配置文件行为，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-4d7a5dc78b3c591e\">权限</a>；有关要求、下发方式和优先级，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">托管配置</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"related-docs\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_7\">相关文档</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-811aa7aeaf6b8cd4\">管理员推广指南</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-10dd8667120ae18c\">群组与用户配置</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-16cf27f48eb09b1c\">用户生命周期管理</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-7d04f9ab1f4a3eeb\">工作区模型可用性</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-f5bc151215cdfb88\">访问令牌</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">托管配置</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-1454a3aad1fa2115\">身份验证</a></li> </ul>","en":"<h1 id=\"roles-and-workspace-permissions\">Roles and workspace permissions</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <p>Different settings cover different parts of your organization's ChatGPT experience. Giving someone access in one area doesn't automatically give them access in another. Use this page to see how the six control boundaries work together, then follow the linked guidance for current setup steps.</p> <p>In workspace settings, <strong>Codex and Work Local</strong> combines local Codex and Work access under <strong>Allow members to use Codex and Work Locally</strong>. Other workspaces separate <strong>Codex Local</strong> and <strong>Work Local</strong> into independent sections. In that layout, <strong>Allow members to use Codex locally</strong> grants local Codex access, and <strong>Use Work locally</strong> grants local Work access. Enabling one doesn't grant access to the other. These labels identify workspace permissions, not separate products or clients. Token permissions and credential lifetime limits appear in either an <strong>Access tokens</strong> section or the local-access section, depending on the workspace. Managed configuration is a separate layer that constrains supported runtime behavior for covered capabilities in those clients. Features and effective requirements can differ by client and version.</p> <h2 id=\"understand-the-control-boundaries\">Understand the control boundaries</h2> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Boundary</th> <th>What it controls</th> <th>What it doesn't control</th> <th>Current source</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>ChatGPT workspace</td> <td>Membership, seats, built-in administration roles, and role-based access to supported workspace features</td> <td>Local agent permissions, Platform API organization access, or permissions in a connected service</td> <td><a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8266401-managing-members-seat-types-roles-and-access-in-chatgpt-enterprise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ChatGPT workspace access (online)</a> and <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/11750701-rbac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RBAC (online)</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Local clients</td> <td>Runtime behavior for covered capabilities in the ChatGPT desktop app, Codex CLI, and IDE extension, including approvals, filesystem and network access, permission profiles, and allowed integrations</td> <td>A ChatGPT seat, feature or model entitlement, or access to external data</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a> and <a href=\"#/article/a-4d7a5dc78b3c591e\">Permissions</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Codex cloud</td> <td>Eligibility to use hosted Codex workflows and the cloud environments made available to the user</td> <td>Local runtime policy or the repository permissions granted by a source system</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1\">Cloud environments</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Platform API</td> <td>Organization and project membership, API keys, model access, usage, and billing for API-authenticated work</td> <td>ChatGPT workspace membership, local-client access, or Codex cloud access</td> <td><a href=\"https://platform.openai.com/docs/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenAI API Platform (online)</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Plugins</td> <td>Plugin availability and installation, bundled skills, connector access, and supported connector actions</td> <td>Authorization in the connected service or broader local and cloud runtime permissions</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-20bc98306a44b420\">Plugin controls</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Connected systems</td> <td>Which repositories, files, messages, and actions the authenticated account can access in the source system</td> <td>ChatGPT workspace, plugin, Codex cloud, or Platform API entitlement</td> <td>The connected service's administration and access controls</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>A request must pass every boundary that applies to it. For example, workspace access can make a plugin available, but the connected service still decides which data the signed-in account can read. A local permission profile can restrict a run in a supported local client, but it can't grant a workspace feature or model.</p> <h2 id=\"assign-workspace-access\">Assign workspace access</h2> <p>ChatGPT workspace administration separates product access from administrative authority.</p> <h3 id=\"understand-the-difference-between-a-seat-an-admin-role-and-a-custom-role\">Understand the difference between a seat, an admin role, and a custom role</h3> <p>A seat determines which product surfaces a member can access. Depending on the workspace plan, available seat types can include ChatGPT and Codex seats.</p> <p>Built-in workspace roles determine administrative authority. The <strong>Owner</strong> role manages workspace-wide settings, the <strong>Admin</strong> role manages supported operations and groups, the <strong>Member</strong> role doesn't have administrative rights, and the <strong>Analytics Viewer</strong> role can access workspace analytics.</p> <p>Custom roles define which supported features a member can use. They don't replace seat or plan eligibility, grant permissions in a connected system, or change local runtime requirements.</p> <p><a href=\"https://player.vimeo.com/video/1215495812\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Embedded media requires online access: Role-based access control walkthrough</a></p> <h3 id=\"set-the-workspace-default-then-create-targeted-custom-roles\">Set the workspace default, then create targeted custom roles</h3> <p>Only workspace owners can configure role-based access control (RBAC) and create custom roles. Workspace settings establish the baseline for eligible permissions. Workspace owners can assign custom roles through groups or directly to individual members where supported. Groups can be manually managed or SCIM-synced, and a member can receive more than one custom role.</p> <p>For eligible permissions, <strong>Default</strong> inherits the workspace setting, <strong>On</strong> grants access through that role, and <strong>Off</strong> does not grant access through that role. Ordinary role permissions combine additively: another assigned role can still grant access. Review direct assignments and roles received through groups. Lockdown Mode and product eligibility apply separately. Available permission states can vary by feature.</p> <h3 id=\"review-work-local-and-work-cloud-permissions\">Review Work Local and Work Cloud permissions</h3> <p>Configure <strong>Cloud browser use</strong> and <strong>Cloud network access</strong> under <strong>Admin Console &gt; Permissions &amp; roles &gt; Workspace capabilities &gt; Cloud computer capabilities</strong>. These shared capabilities are available to Work Cloud and dots and can be configured independently of Work Cloud access. A Work task still needs Work access and permission to use each capability it requires. Review browser access and code or shell network access separately. Disabling one does not automatically disable the other.</p> <p>When your workspace offers <strong>Work Local</strong> and <strong>Work Cloud</strong>, check both the workspace default and each applicable custom role. Work is available only to eligible workspaces, and available controls can differ by plan, workspace configuration, and rollout. A role can't expand the access allowed by a member's seat.</p> <p><strong>Work Cloud</strong> governs supported ChatGPT Work tasks in the cloud. When the controls are independent, <strong>Work Local</strong> without <strong>Work Cloud</strong> allows local work in the ChatGPT desktop app but doesn't allow members to start cloud tasks. Local Codex access uses <strong>Allow members to use Codex locally</strong> in <strong>Codex Local</strong>. Changing <strong>Use Work locally</strong> doesn't change local Codex access or replace local runtime requirements.</p> <p>Some workspaces instead show the combined <strong>Codex and Work Local</strong> section. In that layout, <strong>Allow members to use Codex and Work Locally</strong> controls both products.</p> <p>For current eligibility and settings, see <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001275-chatgpt-work-and-codex\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ChatGPT Work and Codex (online)</a>.</p> <p>Because available seats, roles, and permissions change with product and plan updates, use the Help Center for the current permission list and setup procedure:</p> <ul> <li><a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8266401-managing-members-seat-types-roles-and-access-in-chatgpt-enterprise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Manage members, seat types, roles, and access (online)</a></li> <li><a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/11750701-rbac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Configure role-based access control (online)</a></li> <li><a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/9083985-group-permissions-in-gpts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Manage groups (online)</a></li> </ul> <h3 id=\"review-codex-cloud-access-and-environment-administration\">Review Codex Cloud access and environment administration</h3> <p>Review these permissions separately:</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Permission</th> <th>What it controls</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><strong>Use Codex in the cloud</strong></td> <td>Access to run tasks in Codex Cloud</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Manage workspace environments</strong></td> <td>Creating and editing environments shared with the workspace</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p><strong>Manage workspace environments</strong> sits under <strong>Use Codex in the cloud</strong>, requires Cloud access, and is off by default. Review its grants before rollout. Members with Cloud access can create and edit their own personal environments without the management permission. Access to a shared environment doesn't by itself grant authority to change its configuration.</p> <p>Verify both workflows with representative identities: a member using an environment and the person responsible for managing it. Each still needs the appropriate access in connected repositories and services. See <a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1\">Cloud environments</a> for setup and sharing, and the <a href=\"#/article/a-811aa7aeaf6b8cd4?anchor=step-5-configure-codex-cloud\">admin rollout guide</a> for cloud policy planning.</p> <h3 id=\"local-computer-access-with-work-cloud\">Local computer access with Work Cloud</h3> <p>A workspace owner can enable <strong>Allow local computer access</strong> for eligible users. Enable Work Cloud for the intended users. <strong>Allow local computer access</strong> is nested under Work Cloud. You do not need to enable <strong>Use Codex locally on the ChatGPT desktop app</strong>. Members must sign in with ChatGPT to the intended workspace. If <code>enforce_residency</code> is enabled in any cloud policy, <strong>Allow local computer access</strong> is disabled for both Work and dots. This safeguard does not configure workspace residency or, by itself, disable Work Cloud or dots.</p> <p>Before enabling sync, review Agent Security requirements for local execution. If you currently deliver policies only through MDM, review the applicable local policy order before configuring Agent Security. For local execution, MDM and legacy managed-device requirements rank above Agent Security, while the system requirements file ranks below it. Supported Global policy governs cloud orchestration when managed policy is enabled; local execution requirements govern the connected computer. Work cloud containers retain existing Work Cloud policies.</p> <p>Use the policy API to manage Global settings. To manage Local or Codex Cloud settings, use the Agent Security UI. Existing Global API workflows remain available after migration. Test your scripts and Terraform integrations, and confirm that policy assignments and ordering are unchanged.</p> <p>Set and verify access:</p> <ul> <li> <p>Keep the workspace default appropriate for the wider organization.</p> </li> <li> <p>Assign supported custom roles to the users or groups who need access.</p> </li> <li> <p>Review every applicable role. Turning a permission off in one ordinary role does not remove access granted by another.</p> </li> <li> <p>Test access with a member who has the intended permissions.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Tasks using Local computer access with Work Cloud use cloud coordination and can still run local steps through a connected computer. For enterprises, the in-app Local/Cloud toggle and its default remain unchanged at launch. The sync permission does not grant a seat, connected-app access, operating-system permissions, or unrestricted device access. Local Codex permissions and behavior remain separate. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a> for policy scope and the <a href=\"#/article/a-b498643ba7f3888a\">Work admin FAQ</a> for compatibility requirements.</p> <p>Turning off Local computer access with Work Cloud interrupts currently running turns. Users can start a new turn in an existing cloud conversation. That turn automatically uses Work Cloud without access to local files. Turning off sync does not by itself remove the member's workspace access.</p> <h3 id=\"control-computer-history-access\">Control Computer History access</h3> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-f3a0216fc3d2956b\">Computer History</a> is off by default for Business and Enterprise workspaces. Members cannot turn it on until a workspace owner explicitly grants access. Enterprise workspace owners can grant access by role:</p> <ol> <li>Open <a href=\"https://chatgpt.com/admin/settings\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Workspace Settings &gt; Permissions &amp; roles</strong> (online)</a>.</li> <li>Find <strong>Computer History</strong> and choose the workspace role that should have access.</li> <li>Turn on <strong>Enable Computer History</strong> for that role.</li> </ol> <p>This permission only allows assigned members to turn on Computer History; it does not turn on the feature for them. Each member must opt in from the ChatGPT desktop app on macOS and can choose which apps and websites contribute. Members without the required workspace permission cannot enable the feature through local settings.</p> <h2 id=\"apply-local-runtime-policy\">Apply local runtime policy</h2> <p>Local runtime policy constrains covered capabilities in the ChatGPT desktop app, Codex CLI, and IDE extension. Cloud-managed requirements additionally depend on supported ChatGPT sign-in and plan eligibility. Permission profiles and managed requirements can constrain commands, filesystem access, network access, approvals, and other local runtime behavior. They don't change the user's seat, workspace role, model entitlement, or permissions in an external system.</p> <p>Users can select a built-in or custom permission profile when local policy allows it. Administrators can distribute defaults and requirements through the supported managed-configuration channels. See <a href=\"#/article/a-4d7a5dc78b3c591e\">Permissions</a> for profile behavior and <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a> for requirements, delivery, and precedence.</p> <h2 id=\"related-docs\">Related docs</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-811aa7aeaf6b8cd4\">Admin rollout guide</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-10dd8667120ae18c\">Groups and provisioning</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-16cf27f48eb09b1c\">User lifecycle management</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-7d04f9ab1f4a3eeb\">Workspace model availability</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-f5bc151215cdfb88\">Access tokens</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-1454a3aad1fa2115\">Authentication</a></li> </ul>"};
